39:Every accepted command in the initial independent shared-server matrix reached aggregate and projection state. A subsequent replay with the same Project application, same 80 Entity IDs, batches of 16, eight processes/shards and default shared-server settings accepted all 80 but left two without aggregate or projection after 35 seconds and application restart. Their remote command Inbox records were marked delivered. The initial pass was an intermittent success, not evidence that the Project setup avoided the bug. See [the replay](evidence/shared-delivery-trigger/replay80.json) and DEL-02 below.
41:The investigator also reproduced status-filter and nontransactional cleanup failures. Its timing test measured about 29 seconds without notifications at the default interval and 9 seconds with a 10-second override. This was a controlled one-process timing test, not a new two-process release-race reproduction. A separate worker-kill test recovered pending remote work by 70 seconds; the supervisor's 60-second stale-session threshold is distinct from its recovery interval. Recreating the in-memory Delivery-server instance lost eight accepted pending commands; this was component replacement in one coordinator process, not an OS-process crash.
59:MyISAM and Aria were configured with the documented `latin1_bin` layout so an unrelated key-size restriction would not prevent testing. The fresh investigator also encountered MyISAM’s key-size limit when converting a default utf8mb4 `VARCHAR(512)` primary key. Its narrower-key/latin1 adaptations were disposable fixtures, not a recommendation to convert production identifiers. Distinguish this schema constraint from the later cleanup transaction guard. They were not used for the complete Message Board application. Browser protocol checks ran the browser client transport in Node against the actual HTTP gateway; they were not automated browser UI tests.
61:The three suites cover status filtering, duplicate writes, nanosecond pagination, deleted cursors, acknowledgements, competing workers, lease expiry and takeover, retention cleanup, cancellation, tenant separation, database failures during acknowledgement, command delivery to a projection, subscriptions, and persisted state after application restart. Lower-level tests use the published concrete `Delivery` implementation for its diagnostic counters. The retention-stall test separately calls the public `DeliveryBuilder.run()` API.
